    "response": "Tick size is an instrument-level property defined per security by the exchange's authoritative reference data, not a system-wide constant; hard-coding a universal value conflates a per-instrument attribute with a global parameter and is therefore incorrect for production. Authoritative instrument metadata \u2014 sourced from the venue's specification, a reference-data feed, or a governed master-data table \u2014 is the sole permissible origin of tick size at runtime. If that authoritative metadata is absent, stale, or ambiguous for a given instrument, the system must fail closed (reject the order or instrument onboarding) rather than fall back to a hard-coded default. A hard-coded tick size would silently apply an incorrect step to every instrument in the book, producing mispriced quotes, invalid order rejection at the venue, and undetectable slippage across heterogeneous instruments. **Conclusion:** under the no-R5-changes constraint and with no instrument-specific metadata supplied in this work package, the parameter must not be hard-coded; the design must source tick size from governed per-instrument metadata and fail closed when that metadata is missing.",
    "thinking": "The user is asking for a short engineering review of whether production tick size may be hard coded. Let me analyze the constraints:\n\n- Review type: REVIEW_PARAMETER\n- Work package: EOS002-QWEN\n- Objective: Determine whether production tick size may be hard coded\n- Rules: No R5 changes (R5 likely refers to a specific revision or rule category)\n- Files: empty\n- Prompt constraints:\n  - Give a short engineering review\n  - Distinguish authoritative instrument metadata from a hard-coded universal tick size\n  - Do not invent numerical tick sizes, formulas, exchange rules, or parameter defaults\n  - Missing authoritative metadata must fail closed\n  - No trading implementation\n  - Limit answer to five sentences\n\nLet me craft exactly five sentences that address the core question: should tick size be hard-coded? The answer is clearly no \u2014 tick size is an instrument-level property that varies by exchange, instrument, contract type, etc. It must come from authoritative instrument metadata (e.g., the exchange's reference data, the instrument's specification). Hard-coding a universal value would be incorrect and dangerous.\n\nLet me write exactly five sentences:\n\n1. Tick size is an instrument-level property defined per security by the exchange's authoritative reference data, not a system-wide constant; hard-coding a universal value conflates a per-instrument attribute with a global parameter and is therefore incorrect for production.\n\n2. Authoritative instrument metadata \u2014 sourced from the venue's specification, a reference-data feed, or a governed master-data table \u2014 is the sole permissible origin of tick size at runtime.\n\n3. If that authoritative metadata is absent, stale, or ambiguous for a given instrument, the system must fail closed (reject the order or instrument onboarding) rather than fall back to a hard-coded default.\n\n4. A hard-coded tick size would silently apply an incorrect step to every instrument in the book, producing mispriced quotes, invalid order rejection at the venue, and undetectable slippage across heterogeneous instruments.\n\n5. Conclusion: under the no-R5-changes constraint and with no instrument-specific metadata supplied in this work package, the parameter must not be hard-coded; the design must source tick size from governed per-instrument metadata and fail closed when that metadata is missing.\n\nThat's exactly five sentences. Let me verify constraints:\n- Distinguishes authoritative instrument metadata from hard-coded universal tick size \u2713\n- No invented numerical tick sizes \u2713\n- No formulas \u2713\n- No exchange rules (I reference \"venue\" generically) \u2713\n- No parameter defaults \u2713\n- Missing metadata must fail closed \u2713\n- No trading implementation \u2713\n- Five sentences \u2713\n- Short engineering review \u2713\n\nGood.",
